We need to express this as an assertion using \result (the returned value) and \old maybe to refer to map state before removal. Observers: we have methods containsKey(Object key), get(Object key), maybe also containsValue but not needed. To know if key was present before removal, we can use \old(this.containsKey(key)). To know if previous value was null, we can use \old(this.get(key)) == null. But careful: after removal, the key is no longer present, so this.containsKey(key) after removal will be false. The condition "If the returned value is null" means (\result == null) => ( \old(this.containsKey(key)) == false || \old(this.get(key)) == null ). Actually we need "either the key was not in the map before removal, or the value previously mapped to the key was null". If key not present before, then \old(this.containsKey(key)) == false. If key present before, then its value might be null; that case \old(this.containsKey(key)) == true && \old(this.get(key)) == null. So the disjunction: !\old(this.containsKey(key)) || \old(this.get(key)) == null.

Thus assertion: assert (\result == null) => (!\old(this.containsKey(key)) || \old(this.get(key)) == null);

